\description{
A widget that renders the bio.viz stratified survival chart: a Kaplan-Meier
curve for each group on one endpoint of an outcomes table, with the number
at risk beneath, and under them R's log-rank test, each group's median
survival and the hazard ratio. The groups are a column, or a biomarker or a
participant-level number cut into groups by the shared cut rule. The test is
computed here, in R, by \code{\link[=Analyze_Survival]{Analyze_Survival()}}, and shipped with the page, so
a saved page shows it with no R and no network. A click on a curve, or on a
count at risk, lists those participants.
}
\section{The outcomes table}{

One row per participant and endpoint, with a time and a flag, as ADaM's
time-to-event dataset holds it: \code{PARAMCD} names the endpoint, \code{PARAM} labels
it, \code{AVAL} is the time and \code{CNSR} the flag, 1 for censored. The settings
\code{endpoint_col}, \code{endpoint_label_col}, \code{time_col}, \code{censor_col} and
\code{outcome_id_col} (the participant's id, \code{id_col} when \code{NULL}) name other
columns. A flag the other way round, 1 for an event, is named by
\code{event_col}; named alone it is the flag, and exactly one of the two is
named. A participant with no row for the endpoint, more than one, a time or
flag that is missing or not a number, a flag that is not 0 or 1, or a
negative time is left out, and the chart says how many and why.
}

\section{What the page opens on}{

The endpoint is \code{endpoint}, the first the outcomes table has when none is
named. The groups are \code{group_by}: a column's name, or a cut variable,
\code{list(measure, visit, cut)} for a biomarker or
\code{list(col, type = "number", cut)} for a number, cut at its \code{"median"},
\code{"tertiles"}, \code{"quartiles"} or at typed points; with none named, the first
category column. A column's groups are in order of name; a cut's run low to
high, labelled with their bounds. The setting \code{cuts} lists more cut
variables the Groups control offers, and a cut line can be dragged on the
chart's histogram.
}

\section{Statistics shipped with the page}{

The chart computes no test. It asks R once for its curves, with one row per
participant drawn: the id, the time, the group and the flag. A cut's groups
are handed to R high to low, so the hazard ratio is the higher group's
hazard over the lower's; a column's are in the legend's order, by name with
numbers as numbers (so "2 mg" comes before "10 mg"). The widget
stores R's answer for the view the settings open on. A reader who moves the
endpoint, the groups, a cut line or a filter to a view that was not computed
is told that statistics are unavailable for it; the page never shows one
view's test under another.
}

\section{The cut rule}{

A cut is the one bio.viz uses in every chart: the points are \code{\link[stats:quantile]{stats::quantile()}}
with its default, type 7, on the participants the filters keep who have a
value, or the typed points as written; a participant is in the group
\code{\link[base:cut]{base::cut()}} puts them in with \code{right = TRUE}, so a value equal to a point
falls in the lower group; a bound is written to four significant digits.
}

\section{Filters}{

With a participant table the chart has filters, set by the setting
\code{filters} under safety.viz's rules: a filter opens on its \code{start} when the
data has it and otherwise on All, a filter set \code{all = FALSE} has no All and
opens on its first value, and \code{multiple = TRUE} lets several values through.
R works out what each filter opens on as the chart does, and stores the
results for those participants.

The first value of an \code{all = FALSE} filter is the one exception, because
the chart lists a filter's values in the order of the reader's browser,
which R cannot know: for a letter with an accent or for punctuation it can
differ from R's order, by code point. So the widget hands the chart R's
first value as the filter's \code{start}, and the page opens on the participants
R computed for, though that value may not be the first in the list.
}

\section{Bundles}{

The widget loads bio.viz's bundle and the copy of safety.viz's bundle that
bio.viz itself builds its chart from. Both are copied from bio.viz, with the
bio.viz commit and a checksum per file recorded beside them in
\code{system.file("htmlwidgets", "lib", "SOURCE.json", package = "gsm.bio")}. They
are bio.viz v0.2.0 and safety.viz v1.9.0, the first safety.viz with the kit
the chart is built from, as bio.viz takes it from safety.viz's \code{dev} branch;
the record says from which commit. gsm.safety carries an earlier safety.viz
without the kit, and once it carries v1.9.0 the widgets can take the bundle
from there.
}

